%I A400615 #7 Oct 02 2026 15:40:09
%S A400615 0,1,2,4,6,8,10,12,14,17,19,22,25
%N A400615 Maximum number of edges in the Hasse diagram of a lattice with n elements.
%C A400615 Equivalently, the maximum number of covering pairs in a lattice with n elements.
%C A400615 For any n>2, it is always possible to construct the n-element lattice that has n-2 atoms which are also co-atoms. This construction provides a lower bound of 2(n-2) edges. An upper bound is given by A002620, which counts the maximum number of covering pairs in a poset.  A different upper bound was found by Bollobás and Rival (1979).
%C A400615 It is tempting to think that a(n) might be the number of covering pairs in the n-element Boolean lattice whenever n is a power of two. That this is not the case can be seen from the lattice of subspaces of the Fano plane, which has 16 elements and 35 covering pairs, while the 16-element Boolean lattice only has 32 such pairs.
%H A400615 Béla Bollobás and Ivan Rival, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02488048">The maximal size of the covering graph of a lattice</a>, Algebra Universalis 9 (1979), pp. 371-373.
%e A400615 The first lattice with more than 2 elements that exceeds the bound of 2(n-2) edges has 10 elements and a Hasse diagram with 17 edges. It can be built from the following edge set: (0,1), (0,2), (0,3), (0,4), (1,5), (1,6), (1,7), (2,5), (2,8), (3,6), (3,8), (4,7), (4,8), (5,9), (6,9), (7,9), (8,9).
%Y A400615 Cf. A002620.
%K A400615 hard,nonn,more,new
%O A400615 1,3
%A A400615 _Jakob Süskind_, Oct 01 2026
